        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "0b8d6415-a57c-58e7-a15c-ae124d9b2ce2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Croydon Airport, Gatwick Airport, Earlswood, London, South London, south-east England, Central London, Crawley, Redhill, Horley, Whitebushes\n- Airports: Northolt Aerodrome, London Heathrow Airport, Brussels-Evere Airport\n- Codes: EGCR, LGW, EGKK\n- Events: 1921 SNETA Farman Goliath ditching\n- Topics: airport replacement, airport traffic ranking, mail flight routes\n\nFacts:\n- Croydon Airport, with ICAO code EGCR, was an airport in South London, England.\n- Croydon Airport was London's main airport before Northolt Aerodrome, London Heathrow Airport, and Gatwick Airport replaced it.\n- The aircraft in the 1921 SNETA Farman Goliath ditching was operating a mail flight from Croydon Airport in the United Kingdom to Brussels-Evere Airport in Belgium.\n- Gatwick Airport, also known as London Gatwick, has IATA code LGW and ICAO code EGKK.\n- Gatwick Airport is a major international airport in south-east England, 29.5 miles south of Central London and 2.7 nautical miles north of Crawley.\n- Gatwick Airport is the eighth-busiest airport in Europe.\n- Earlswood lies on the A23 between Redhill, in the direction of London, and Horley, which is next to Gatwick Airport.\n- Whitebushes is separated from Earlswood by a wide rural buffer zone.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a34d7220-06d6-5ae9-8f7b-d3d7c9f9276b",
